    "summary": "High‑energy Halloween show with smoking Dark Star and crisp Keith piano",
    "blurb": "The set features a blazing Dark Star opening the second set and an inspired El Paso in the first. Jerry’s improvisational riffs shine while Phil anchors with solid bass and adds vocal harmonies on Jack Straw. Keith’s fresh piano texture rounds out a tight, high‑energy performance.",
    "review": "October 31, 1971 at the Ohio Theatre captures the Dead at a Halloween peak, delivering a night that feels both spontaneous and meticulously locked in. The first set rolls through classics with an especially gripping El Paso that lets Garcia stretch his improvisational muscles, while Phil’s bass keeps everything grounded and Bob adds reliable rhythm work. After a solid first half, the second set erupts with a smoking Dark Star that opens a cascade of seamless segues—Not Fade Away into Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad—showcasing the band’s uncanny ability to keep momentum blazing. Keith Godchaux’s piano is fresh and crisp throughout, adding bright color even when the early mix pushes it forward. The encore crowns the night with a rollicking Johnny B. Goode, leaving the Columbus crowd buzzing. With two excellent soundboard sources that capture the full show in clear detail, this Halloween performance stands out as one of the season’s most electric outings.",
